# Specification Standards

<version>0.1.0</version>

## Context

miniforge uses 6 normative specifications (N1-N6) that define ALL contractual requirements. All
implementation MUST conform to these specs. Specs extracted from strategic vision, NOT from code.

**Entry point:** `specs/SPEC_INDEX.md`

## Hierarchy

```zsh
specs/
├── SPEC_INDEX.md          # START HERE - authoritative entry point
├── README.md              # Implementation guide
├── normative/             # N1-N6 (MUST/SHALL only)
├── informative/           # Guidance (no MUST/SHALL)
├── examples/              # Reference implementations
└── deprecated/            # Superseded (ignore)
```

## Requirements

### When Reading Specs

<required>
- ALWAYS start with `specs/SPEC_INDEX.md` for overview
- Read normative specs (N1-N6) for MUST/SHALL requirements
- NEVER use deprecated/ directory content
- Cross-reference: specs link to each other
- Use examples/ for concrete patterns
</required>

### When Implementing

<required>
- Implementation MUST conform to all 6 normative specs
- Event emission MUST follow N3 (Event Stream)
- Evidence bundles MUST follow N6 (Evidence & Provenance)
- Workflow execution MUST follow N2 (Workflows)
- Policy packs MUST follow N4 (Policy Packs)
- CLI/TUI/API MUST follow N5 (Interface Standard)
- Architecture MUST follow N1 (Core Architecture)
</required>

### When Amending Specs

<required>
- NO new normative spec files (only amend N1-N6)
- New concepts MUST land in N1 or they're not real
- New runtime data MUST land in N3 or N6 or it's unobservable
- New UX features MUST point to contract in N3/N5/N6
- Normative specs MUST use RFC 2119 keywords (MUST/SHALL/SHOULD/MAY)
- Informative docs MUST NOT use RFC 2119 keywords
</required>

### Spec Extraction Source

<critical>
- Extract specs from strategic documents (software-factory-vision.md, oss-paid-roadmap.md)
- NEVER extract specs from implementation code
- Specs define what MUST be built, not what happens to exist
- Code conforms to specs, not the reverse
</critical>

## Six Normative Specs

| Spec | File | Purpose | Key Contracts |
|------|------|---------|---------------|
| N1 | `N1-architecture.md` | Core concepts, layering | 12 nouns, 3-layer architecture, Polylith boundaries |
| N2 | `N2-workflows.md` | Workflow execution | Phase graph, inner/outer loops, gate contract |
| N3 | `N3-event-stream.md` | Event stream & observability | 9 event types, SSE/WebSocket, ordering guarantees |
| N4 | `N4-policy-packs.md` | Policy packs & gates | Semantic intent validation, check/repair contract |
| N5 | `N5-cli-tui-api.md` | CLI/TUI/API interface | Command taxonomy, TUI views, streaming endpoints |
| N6 | `N6-evidence-provenance.md` | Evidence & provenance | Evidence bundles, artifact provenance, semantic validation |

**Most leverageful:** N3 (Event Stream) and N6 (Evidence & Provenance) - everything builds on these.

## Examples

<example>
✅ GOOD: Implementing event emission

```clojure
;; Following N3 event schema
(emit-event {:event/type :agent/status
             :event/id (uuid)
             :event/timestamp (Instant/now)
             :event/version "1.0.0"
             :workflow/id workflow-id
             :event/sequence-number (next-seq)
             :agent/id :implementer
             :status/type :generating
             :message "Generating Terraform code..."})
```

Agent emits events following N3 schema exactly.
</example>

<example type="valid">
✅ GOOD: Semantic intent validation

Semantic validation catches intent violations before any resource change is committed.

```clojure
;; Following N6 semantic validation
(validate-intent {:declared :import
                  :actual-creates 0
                  :actual-destroys 0})
;; => {:passed? true}

(validate-intent {:declared :import
                  :actual-creates 3   ; VIOLATION
                  :actual-destroys 0})
;; => {:passed? false :violation "Declared IMPORT but creates 3 resources"}
```
</example>

<example type="invalid">
❌ BAD: Extracting spec from code

```clojure
;; Reading implementation to write spec
(read-file "miniforge/components/agent/core.clj")
;; Then writing spec based on what code does
```

NEVER extract specs from code. Extract from strategic documents (software-factory-vision.md, oss-paid-roadmap.md).
</example>

<example type="invalid">
❌ BAD: Creating new normative spec

```zsh
specs/normative/N7-new-feature.md  # WRONG!
```

NO new normative specs. Amend existing N1-N6 only.
</example>

